// E0443: a `.plyx` whose bytes are not the bytes it claims.
//
// There is nothing wrong with this program, and there is nothing a program could
// write to produce this code — which is the point of the fixture. An artifact is
// a set of definition bodies filed under their own hashes, so the integrity
// check is the hash rather than a checksum bolted beside it, and every way a
// transfer can go wrong is refused per definition before anything runs:
//
//   $ ply build artifact_invalid.ply -o app.plyx
//   $ printf '\x01' | dd of=app.plyx bs=1 seek=299 conv=notrunc
//   $ ply run app.plyx
//   E0443  the definition filed under `e2bf50fc87f4` at offset 260 is not the
//          definition that hash names
//          = a body is a function of its hash, so this is corruption rather
//            than a difference of opinion
//
//   $ head -c 300 app.plyx > truncated.plyx && ply run truncated.plyx
//   E0443  section 1 claims bytes 260..475 of a 300-byte file
//
// A body naming a hash the `BODIES` section does not hold is the same code and a
// different sentence: the closure was computed wrongly, or the file is short.
//
// The response to `E0443` is "transfer it again"; the response to `E0444` is
// "rebuild it". That is why they are two codes rather than one.

pub fn shade(n: Int) -> Int = n + n

fn main() -> Int = shade(21)
